atom	desires_general(x)	x has an actual desire for the general happiness
atom	ought_aim_general	each individual ought to desire, or aim at realising in action, the general happiness
atom	means_ought	by "the general happiness is desirable" Mill means that it is what each individual ought to desire or aim at
atom	established	the proposition that the general happiness is desirable (in the ought sense) is established by Mill's reasoning
atom	gap	there is a gap in Mill's expressed argument
atom	only_benevolence_fills	the gap can only be filled by some such proposition as the intuition of Rational Benevolence
# gh = the general happiness; happiness = happiness in general
# "desirable" shifts sense: in S7 it means capable of being desired (proven_desirable_cap); in S8 and S11 it means worth desiring, what ought to be desired (reason_desirable_good, means_ought)
P1	S7	∀x (proven_desirable_cap(x) → desired(x))	says mill
P2	S8	reason_desirable_good(gh) → ∀x (person(x) → desires_own_happiness(x))	says mill
P3	S9	∀x (person(x) → desires_own_happiness(x)) → full_proof_good(happiness) ∧ ∀x (person(x) → own_happiness_good(x)) ∧ good_to_aggregate(gh)	says mill
P4	S11	means_ought	kind conceptual
P5	S13	∀x (person(x) → ¬desires_general(x))
P6	S14	∀x (person(x) → ¬desires_general(x)) → ¬established
P7	S14	¬established → gap
P8	S14	only_benevolence_fills
C1	S12	¬established	from P5 P6
C	S14	gap	from C1 P7
